            <ArticleTitle>Household conditions, eczema symptoms and rhinitis symptoms: relationship with wheeze and severe wheeze in adolescents living in the Polokwane area, South Africa.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>659-66</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Determine the prevalence and risk factors of wheeze and severe wheeze in 13-to 14-year-old children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study was conducted August 2004 to February 2005 in the Polokwane area, South Africa.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The 12-month prevalence rate was 18.9% for wheeze and 9.2% for severe wheeze (n = 3,926). The presence of other allergic symptoms and industrial activities appear to increase the likelihood of wheeze, even more so for severe wheeze. Socioeconomic-related factors appear to have a protective effect on wheeze.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Wheeze appears to be a substantial public health problem in the Polokwane area.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
